1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,480
Mo thinks he's a nobody.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:04,510
Just a guy who makes tuna melts.

3
00:00:04,735 --> 00:00:06,875
He has no idea that half the houses on

4
00:00:06,895 --> 00:00:08,825
this island exist because of him.

5
00:00:09,981 --> 00:00:10,841
This is Mo.

6
00:00:10,861 --> 00:00:12,521
Twenty-two years of sandwiches.

7
00:00:12,541 --> 00:00:13,401
Same shop.

8
00:00:13,421 --> 00:00:14,281
Same apron.

9
00:00:14,301 --> 00:00:15,561
A life he'd call...

10
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:16,431
small.

11
00:00:16,611 --> 00:00:17,951
Then, one Tuesday night,

12
00:00:17,971 --> 00:00:19,151
while he was locking up,

13
00:00:19,171 --> 00:00:20,541
Mo started to glow.

14
00:00:20,887 --> 00:00:21,907
Golden threads.

15
00:00:21,927 --> 00:00:22,867
Hundreds of them.

16
00:00:22,887 --> 00:00:24,497
And they came with rules.

17
00:00:24,743 --> 00:00:27,283
One: every thread is a life you've touched.

18
00:00:27,303 --> 00:00:28,963
Two: the brighter the thread,

19
00:00:28,983 --> 00:00:30,563
the more it mattered to them.

20
00:00:30,583 --> 00:00:32,083
Three: pull a thread,

21
00:00:32,103 --> 00:00:34,003
and that moment never happened.

22
00:00:34,023 --> 00:00:36,113
Rule three should have come with a warning.

23
00:00:37,264 --> 00:00:39,914
The brightest thread led somewhere Mo didn't expect.

24
00:00:40,252 --> 00:00:40,872
Theo.

25
00:00:40,892 --> 00:00:43,512
Last spring, his very first week on the island.

26
00:00:43,532 --> 00:00:44,312
Lonely.

27
00:00:44,332 --> 00:00:44,872
Hungry.

28
00:00:44,892 --> 00:00:46,182
Counting coins.

29
00:00:46,333 --> 00:00:47,273
MO: On the house.

30
00:00:47,293 --> 00:00:48,663
Welcome to the island.

31
00:00:49,137 --> 00:00:50,957
Mo forgot about it by lunchtime.

32
00:00:50,977 --> 00:00:52,347
Theo never did.

33
00:00:52,688 --> 00:00:54,348
Turns out, that's normal.

34
00:00:54,368 --> 00:00:57,388
In one study, people handed out free hot chocolate

35
00:00:57,408 --> 00:00:59,068
at a winter skating rink.

36
00:00:59,088 --> 00:01:01,308
The givers guessed it would mean a little.

37
00:01:01,328 --> 00:01:03,148
The people who got it said it meant a

38
00:01:03,168 --> 00:01:04,018
lot more.

39
00:01:04,160 --> 00:01:07,260
We tend to underestimate how much our small kindnesses

40
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:08,130
matter.

41
00:01:08,765 --> 00:01:10,695
And the thread didn't stop at Theo.

42
00:01:10,751 --> 00:01:13,371
That sandwich was the first kind thing anyone did

43
00:01:13,391 --> 00:01:14,571
for Theo here.

44
00:01:14,591 --> 00:01:15,931
It wasn't the last.

45
00:01:15,951 --> 00:01:19,371
These days, Theo says "Morning!" to everyone.

46
00:01:19,391 --> 00:01:21,771
When Bea the baker dropped a whole tower of

47
00:01:21,791 --> 00:01:23,771
trays, he was the first to help.

48
00:01:23,791 --> 00:01:26,171
And Bea started baking an extra batch for her

49
00:01:26,191 --> 00:01:27,721
neighbour, who lives alone.

50
00:01:28,004 --> 00:01:28,944
One sandwich.

51
00:01:28,964 --> 00:01:30,014
Three lives.

52
00:01:30,417 --> 00:01:32,317
In a lab game with real money,

53
00:01:32,337 --> 00:01:34,317
when one player gave a little more,

54
00:01:34,337 --> 00:01:37,437
people two and even three steps away started giving

55
00:01:37,457 --> 00:01:38,317
more too.

56
00:01:38,337 --> 00:01:40,267
People who had never even met them.

57
00:01:41,203 --> 00:01:43,423
Then Mo found the thickest thread of all.

58
00:01:43,443 --> 00:01:45,533
It ran all the way down to the harbour.

59
00:01:45,827 --> 00:01:47,087
Eleven years ago.

60
00:01:47,107 --> 00:01:48,047
A storm.

61
00:01:48,067 --> 00:01:50,207
A soaked stranger with a suitcase,

62
00:01:50,227 --> 00:01:52,557
waiting for the last boat off the island.

63
00:01:52,697 --> 00:01:53,797
MO: We're closed, but...

64
00:01:53,817 --> 00:01:54,437
come in.

65
00:01:54,457 --> 00:01:55,907
The soup's still hot.

66
00:01:56,248 --> 00:01:57,748
That stranger was Kai.

67
00:01:57,768 --> 00:01:58,868
He missed the boat.

68
00:01:58,888 --> 00:01:59,668
He stayed.

69
00:01:59,688 --> 00:02:02,098
And he built half the houses on this island.

70
00:02:02,587 --> 00:02:04,727
Mo wondered what the island would look like without

71
00:02:04,747 --> 00:02:05,847
that one night.

72
00:02:05,867 --> 00:02:06,967
Just for a second.

73
00:02:06,987 --> 00:02:08,037
So he pulled.

74
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:10,520
The houses.

75
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:11,240
The porches.

76
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:12,600
The families inside them.

77
00:02:12,620 --> 00:02:13,470
Gone.

78
00:02:13,649 --> 00:02:14,619
MO: No, no, no...

79
00:02:15,292 --> 00:02:17,272
Mo pushed the thread back as fast as he

80
00:02:17,292 --> 00:02:18,142
could.

81
00:02:19,188 --> 00:02:21,888
There's an experiment about this exact feeling.

82
00:02:21,908 --> 00:02:24,048
People wrote about a good thing in their life,

83
00:02:24,068 --> 00:02:26,638
but imagined how it might never have happened.

84
00:02:26,784 --> 00:02:29,244
It lifted their mood more than writing about how

85
00:02:29,264 --> 00:02:30,364
it did happen.

86
00:02:30,384 --> 00:02:32,474
Most people expected the opposite.

87
00:02:32,831 --> 00:02:35,691
Sometimes you only see what something means when you

88
00:02:35,711 --> 00:02:36,841
picture it gone.

89
00:02:38,092 --> 00:02:40,312
By sunrise, the threads had faded.

90
00:02:40,332 --> 00:02:42,582
Mo opened the shop like any other day.

91
00:02:43,165 --> 00:02:44,345
THEO: I never said thank you.

92
00:02:44,365 --> 00:02:44,665
So...

93
00:02:44,685 --> 00:02:45,815
I wrote it down.

94
00:02:46,283 --> 00:02:47,863
The note was three lines long.

95
00:02:47,883 --> 00:02:49,573
Mo read it four times.

96
00:02:50,019 --> 00:02:51,679
And one last thread lit up.

97
00:02:51,699 --> 00:02:52,719
Pointing the other way.

98
00:02:52,739 --> 00:02:53,629
Toward him.

99
00:02:54,696 --> 00:02:56,116
You'll never see your threads.

100
00:02:56,136 --> 00:02:57,236
But they're there.

101
00:02:57,256 --> 00:03:00,116
So try this: think of one person who mattered

102
00:03:00,136 --> 00:03:01,556
to you, and tell them.

103
00:03:01,576 --> 00:03:03,396
In studies of thank-you letters,

104
00:03:03,416 --> 00:03:06,516
writers underestimated how good it made the other person

105
00:03:06,536 --> 00:03:09,666
feel, and overestimated how awkward it would be.

106
00:03:10,780 --> 00:03:12,440
Mo thought his life was small.

107
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:13,980
Turns out, nobody's is.
